    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm looking at an NDA for a new contract and I'm a bit stuck on the terminology. Can you explain what a \"residuals clause\" usually covers? Specifically, if I learn a certain way to optimize database queries while working for them, does this clause let me use those same general logic patterns for other clients later, or am I stuck? \n\nHere is the text: \"Notwithstanding anything to the contrary in this Agreement, the Receiving Party shall be free to use for any purpose the Residuals resulting from access to or use of the Confidential Information, provided that the Receiving Party shall not disclose the Confidential Information itself. The term 'Residuals' means information in non-tangible form, which may be retained in the unaided memory of persons who have had access to the Confidential Information.\"",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A residuals clause, as you've quoted, is a common legal safeguard against the idea that you have to keep *every* stray thought about the confidential information locked up forever. It's designed to allow you to retain general knowledge, like the abstract concepts or \"know-how,\" that you absorb while working on a project—things that aren't written down in a document. In your scenario, this clause seems to permit you to use the *general logic patterns* you learned for optimizing database queries, provided you don't reveal the specific, proprietary database structure or data itself.\n\nThink of it this way: the company can't stop you from remembering *how* to efficiently sort a large dataset, but they *can* stop you from handing over the actual, secret blueprint of their specific database. It's a narrow carve-out for your own retained skill set. If the clause is worded as you presented, it generally allows you to utilize those general, unaided memories for future work, even with other clients.\n\nIt's important to note that while this clause is favorable to you, you should still have a lawyer review the entire NDA, because sometimes the definition of \"Confidential Information\" can be written",
      "source": "target",
